are required, and the bot refuses to start without them, naming whichever is
missing (see `.env.example`; `.env` is git-ignored). Mention the bot in any
channel it is in (`@your-bot ping`) and it replies `pong`.

`register_listeners()` is split from `build_app()`/`main()`:
`tests/test_bot_slack.py` registers the listeners on a recording stand-in and
drives the mention listener with a fake `say`, so the bot is tested
in-process with no socket, no token and no WebClient.
{% endif %}{% if bot_line_effective %}
The project also ships a **LINE bot** (`{{ import_pkg }}/bot_line.py`,
[line-bot-sdk](https://developers.line.biz/en/docs/messaging-api/)). The
Messaging API has no socket and no long-polling: LINE pushes every event to a
**webhook**, so the bot serves it itself — a small FastAPI app answering
`POST /callback` on `BOT_PORT` (default 8000, all interfaces):

```sh
# put both credentials in .env first (cp .env.example .env)
uv run bot-line-{{ repo_name }}
```

`LINE_CHANNEL_SECRET` verifies the `X-Line-Signature` LINE signs every request
body with, and `LINE_CHANNEL_ACCESS_TOKEN` authorizes the reply; both are
required — the bot refuses to start without either and names the one that is
missing (see `.env.example`; `.env` is git-ignored). Expose the port over
HTTPS (a tunnel is enough while developing) and register
`https://<your host>/callback` as the channel's webhook URL. The signature
check is what makes that public URL safe: an unsigned or forged POST is
rejected before any event is read. Send the bot `ping` and it replies `pong`.

`build_app()` is split from `main()` and takes the reply call as a parameter:
`tests/test_bot_line.py` drives `POST /callback` over httpx's ASGITransport
with a recording reply, so the whole route — signature check included — is
tested in-process, with no token and no network.
{% endif %}{% if bot_gmail_effective %}
The project also ships a **Gmail bot** (`{{ import_pkg }}/bot_gmail.py`,
[google-api-python-client](https://github.com/googleapis/google-api-python-client)
+ google-auth) that polls the INBOX:

```sh
# put both credential paths in .env first (cp .env.example .env)
uv run bot-gmail-{{ repo_name }}
```

`GMAIL_CREDENTIALS_JSON` names the OAuth client downloaded from Google Cloud
Console (APIs & Services → Credentials → OAuth client ID → Desktop app), and
`GMAIL_TOKEN_JSON` names the granted token file the first run writes (the
consent browser opens once; every later run refreshes from disk). Both are
required — the bot refuses to start without either and names the one that is
missing (see `.env.example`; both files stay git-ignored). Every
`GMAIL_POLL_SECONDS` (default 60) it answers an unread INBOX message whose
subject contains `/ping` with a `pong` reply on the same thread and marks it
read — mail that never says `/ping` is left unread.

`poll_once()` takes the API client as a parameter: `tests/test_bot_gmail.py`
drives the poll with a recording stand-in — the raw RFC 2822 reply is decoded
right in the test — so the bot is tested in-process, with no OAuth, no
network and no mailbox.
