## 🏆 Online-judge workflow

The judge CLIs (`oj`, `acc`, `aoj`, the Kattis client) are **user-installed
tools, not project dependencies** — the workspace is bare on purpose, so a
recipe that reaches for a missing CLI prints an install hint (`pip install
online-judge-tools`, `npm install -g atcoder-cli`,
`go install github.com/YuminosukeSato/AOJ-cli/cmd/aojcli@latest`) and stops,
instead of failing with a stack trace.

Four task-runner recipes drive the daily loop
(`new` → `dl` → edit `main.py` → `sample` → `submit`):

| Recipe | What it does | Example |
|--------|--------------|---------|
| `new <path>` | Scaffold `<path>/main.py` + `<path>/test/` (`acc new <contest>` on AtCoder) | `just new abc086/a` |
| `dl <url>` | Download the samples into `test/` | `just dl https://atcoder.jp/contests/abc086/tasks/abc086_a` |
| `sample <dir>` | Run the judge's sample tests in `<dir>` | `just sample abc086/a` |
| `submit <dir>` | Submit `main.py` from `<dir>` | `just submit abc086/a` |

`sample` runs the judge's *sample* cases; the `test` task is the repo's own
pytest suite (the workspace ships one smoke test, and CI runs it on every
push). The same four recipes exist in every task runner:
`task new -- abc086/a`, `make new DIR=abc086/a`,
`invoke new abc086/a`, `duty new abc086/a`.

What each judge's tooling can and cannot do from the terminal:

| Judge | Tool | `new` | `dl` | `sample` | `submit` |
|-------|------|-------|------|----------|----------|
| AtCoder | [atcoder-cli](https://github.com/Tatamo/atcoder-cli) + [oj](https://github.com/online-judge-tools/oj) | `acc new` | `oj download` | `oj test` | `oj submit` |
| yukicoder | [oj](https://github.com/online-judge-tools/oj) | scaffold | `oj download` | `oj test` | `oj submit` |
| Codeforces | [oj](https://github.com/online-judge-tools/oj) | scaffold | `oj download` | `oj test` | `oj submit` where the service allows, else the browser |
| AOJ | [aoj-cli](https://github.com/YuminosukeSato/AOJ-cli) | scaffold | `aoj init` | `aoj test` | `aoj submit` |
| Kattis | [oj](https://github.com/online-judge-tools/oj) + the [Kattis client](https://github.com/Kattis/kattis-cli) | scaffold | `oj download` | `oj test` | `python3 submit.py` |
| LeetCode | — | scaffold | none — copy the samples into `test/` by hand | none — the LeetCode editor is the judge | the browser editor |
| other | [oj](https://github.com/online-judge-tools/oj) | scaffold | `oj download` | `oj test` | the judge's website / editor |

`oj login <judge-url>` (or `aoj login`) once stores the session the submit
step needs; Kattis's `submit.py` needs a personal `~/.kattisrc` (git-ignored).
{% if oj_sample and is_github %}The optional `oj_sample` workflow
(`.github/workflows/oj-sample.yml`) runs the judge CLI's own sample check on
manual dispatch and a weekly schedule — it needs network and the judge's tool,
so it stays out of `check` and the push CI.
